  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1238 AM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThere is still a LOT of uncertainty among the various model\nsolutions through Thursday with the handling of the remnants of\nPolo and the track of the upper low. The ECMWF continues to\ntrack the upper low further west, while the GFS is further\nsoutheast. There is better agreement beyond Thursday with high\npressure impacting the region over the weekend into early next\nweek.\n\nWednesday through Thursday...as mentioned, there remains a high\ndegree of uncertainty with the upper low storm track, gradient,\nand QPF amounts. There are two scenarios that remain plausible\nduring this period...the ECMWF, some CAM guidance, and several\nensembles tracking the upper low along with the remnants of Polo\nnorth out of New Mexico, and across southern Colorado before\nshifting the system east Thursday evening. These solutions are\nby far the wettest solutions, most with widespread 2 to 4 inch\namounts focused along the central/southern I-25 corridor and\nRaton Mesa region. Strong upper dynamics, deep upslope flow, and\nmoisture wrapping around the system look to produce a period of\nheavy rainfall Wednesday into Thursday morning over the areas\nmentioned above. This activity looks to shift eastward Thursday\nafternoon and clear into Kansas overnight Thursday. Wind in\nthese solutions would be possible Wednesday night Thursday\nmorning, but not as strong as the further east guidance.\n\nLooking at the other scenario, the GFS, NAM 12km and other\nvarious AI and ensemble guidance are clustered around a drier\nsolution. They track the upper low out of New Mexico and into\nthe Central Plains. A majority of the guidance in this camp have\nmuch stronger north downslope winds Wednesday night into\nThursday morning, limiting precipitation amounts along the I-25\ncorridor (very similar to our winter storms). These guidance\nhave the highest totals out east along the Kansas border. QPF\namounts with these solutions vary from a half inch or less, to\naround an inch and a half over the Raton Mesa and near the\nKansas border. In addition, a strong gradient would set up, with\nvery strong winds possible off the Palmer Divide and across the\nPlains north of Highway 50. Wind gusts in excess of 40 to 50 mph\nwould be possible.  \n\nAs for the the forecast...did not stray from the NBM guidance,\nwhich is similar to the ECMWF camp, with widespread 1 to 3\ninches of rainfall during this period, mainly along and south of\nHighway 50, with the highest amounts along the Wet Mountains and\nsouthern Sangre de Cristo Range. Just know that if the storm\ntrack is further east (like the GFS), rainfall amounts could be\nsignificantly less!  \n\nTemperatures do look to remain cool, with 50s and 60s both\nWednesday and Thursday across the region, and 40s to 50s for\novernight lows. A Flash Flood Watch remain in effect for this\nperiod for most of the county warning area, including the Aspen\nAcres burn scar.\n\nSnow levels look to remain high; above 12 kft through this\nperiod. Several inches of snow are possible across the higher\npeaks.\n\nFriday through Tuesday...upper level high pressure looks to\nbuild across the Great Basin over the weekend into early next\nweek, with generally broad northwesterly flow aloft.\nTemperatures look to warm, and much drier air will work into the\nregion. Expect any residual showers on the Plains to have\ncleared by Friday morning, with dry conditions through early\nnext week anticipated. Temperatures will warm back through the\n70s and lower 80s. A couple of dry cold fronts Saturday and\nMonday will bring minor cool downs to the Plains.",
